The main features of diagnostic communication
are:
- the communication channel is the CAN BUS;
- the diagnostic tool is an added node on the
CAN network;
- the diagnostic tool requires for the diagnostic
information to each ECU;
- the interested ECU provides the responses
about the question;
- the translation of the diagnostic information
from raw value to “engineering” value is
performed by specific tools.
CommunicationDiagnostic

All control systems must foresee the following functions:
o managing a physical transmission ;
o recognizing the emergencies on all sensors and actuators directly connected to the system, on the power supply lines both entering
and exiting the system and, if possible, identifying also mechanical part faults and malfunctions through a plausibility diagnosis;
o the self-diagnosis functions must be able to distinguish between failures and disturbances;
o implementing a fault management procedure;
o implementing a recovery strategy for each type of detected emergency;
o transmission of the engineering parameters upon request of the diagnostic instrument;
o controlling the system actuators (active diagnosis) through the specific controls;
o managing the emergency warning lamp(s), if foreseen;
o reprogramming the electronic system;
o storing statistic parameters.
PerformanceDiagnostic

o The error information must be stored in the non-volatile memory;
o The control units that, for legislative and/or active safety reasons, must be able to update
the non-volatile memory content with the data present in RAM also in case of a sudden or
accidental power supply loss;
o System component diagnostics must be operative in all vehicle conditions – KeyOn,
Cranking, Engine Runnig, Vehicle Running, Power Latch (or After Run);
o The electronic system must be able to recognize the cranking phase in order to filter any
“disturbances” that could lead to the validation of false diagnoses;
General rulesRecognition of failures –

o Electrical anomalies (O.C., S.C.G., S.C.B) on the signal and power supply lines of each sensor;
o Electrical anomalies (O.C., S.C.G., S.C.B) on the control and power supply lines of each actuator;
o Signal PLAUSIBILITY: software controls that are used to detect if a signal that originates from an
individual sensor or from the feedback of an actuator is reliable or not;
o S.C.between different sensor/actuator signal lines; S.C. between signal lines and possible reference
voltages;
o if the sensor/actuator power supply is the same for multiple components, a strategy must be foreseen
(to be agreed upon with EE - EAI) that verifies the contemporaneity of a power supply failure on all
sensors/actuators supplied by the same line.
Sensor/Actuator failuresRecognition of failures –

o Node ABSENT – Node A recognizes node C as absent from the network:
- node A controls the reception of only ONE message from node C;
- node A controls the presence of ALL messages transmitted by node C;
o there must be only one DTC assigned to each source node;
o the detection of this error must be disabled during engine cranking;
o the detection time (tr) for this diagnosis must always be greater than the maximum value
between the mute period for the bus-off specified for the source node (tBUS-OFF) and
the network input time of the source node (tPon) to avoid storing multiple DTC codes;
o this diagnosis must be inhibited immediately In case in which the receiving Node detected
a Node Mute error on itself;
o maturation time: for BH and C CAN the minimum validation time is 1s and the maximum
maturation time is 5s.
Node ABSENTPossible CAN failures –

The error memory is composed of cells that contain the following fields:
o DTC Code (DTCMaskRecord): is composed of 3 bytes; the first 2 bytes identify the component and the third byte (DTCLowByte statusOfDTC )
identifies the type of fault (symptom);
o StatusOfDTC: identifies the status of the DTC at the moment of the reading with the diagnostic instrument;
o DTCStatusMask: each bit has the same meaning of the StatusOfDTC but it’s used to require information;
o DTCSnapshotRecordNumber: identifies, in the error memory reading service, the record snapshot among those available in the ECU;
o DTCExtendedDataRecordNumber: identifies, in the error memory reading service, the record snapshot among those available in the ECU;
o DTCSeverityMask: each Supplier agrees with the EE - EAI systems engineer upon the rules for assigning the severity related to each DTC;
o DTCSnapshotRecord: this is made up of environmental parameters recorded at the moment of validating the fault and symptom that generated
the storage.
